WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:04.750
This is the technical deep
dive for Book 08 of the

3
00:00:04.750 --> 00:00:09.137
Federal Modernization
Workbook, DNS, DHCP and IPAM.

4
00:00:09.137 --> 00:00:13.701
It's for the network and DDI engineers
who will design the resolution paths,

5
00:00:13.701 --> 00:00:17.087
move authority off Active
Directory, and run the result.

6
00:00:17.087 --> 00:00:18.649
It comes in two parts.

7
00:00:18.649 --> 00:00:20.789
Part 1 covers the hard surfaces:

8
00:00:20.789 --> 00:00:24.985
split-brain DNS and what Active
Directory DNS really carries,

9
00:00:24.985 --> 00:00:27.782
the places queries escape agency policy,

10
00:00:27.782 --> 00:00:32.800
and the DNS identity and DNSSEC work
underneath certificates and tokens.

11
00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:36.762
Part 2 builds and runs the control
plane: cloud private endpoints,

12
00:00:36.762 --> 00:00:41.164
IPAM as the authority for address
blocks, the reference architecture,

13
00:00:41.164 --> 00:00:45.200
then the six-phase migration and
the evidence that keeps it honest.

14
00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:50.050
Vendors appear only as the book's
examples, never as endorsements.

15
00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:52.187
Here's why this matters.

16
00:00:52.187 --> 00:00:56.775
Almost every connection begins
with a DNS query, and in a hybrid,

17
00:00:56.775 --> 00:01:01.626
multi-cloud agency the answer now
decides far more than an address.

18
00:01:01.626 --> 00:01:08.687
NIST's 2026 DNS guide says DNS has become
a security control, not just a service.

19
00:01:08.687 --> 00:01:10.919
Chapter 01 names six jobs it now does,

20
00:01:10.919 --> 00:01:15.233
from publishing private addresses
for cloud endpoints to anchoring

21
00:01:15.233 --> 00:01:19.250
certificate checks, because
revocation endpoints are names too.

22
00:01:19.250 --> 00:01:25.717
Yet most agencies run DNS on as many
as five platforms that share no state:

23
00:01:25.717 --> 00:01:29.812
on-premises, AWS, Azure,
Google Cloud and VMware.

24
00:01:29.812 --> 00:01:32.337
The cost shows up on every change.

25
00:01:32.337 --> 00:01:36.776
A new Azure private endpoint
can need four more DNS changes,

26
00:01:36.776 --> 00:01:39.312
on four consoles, in four syntaxes.

27
00:01:39.312 --> 00:01:41.789
And the forwarding
rules that connect those

28
00:01:41.789 --> 00:01:44.570
platforms usually belong
to no single team.

29
00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:49.812
The book quotes the mandates rather
than inflating them, and so will I.

30
00:01:49.812 --> 00:01:55.250
Read together, they ask for agency-controlled
resolvers that log, encrypt, validate,

31
00:01:55.250 --> 00:01:59.190
send internet-bound queries
through CISA's Protective DNS,

32
00:01:59.190 --> 00:02:00.845
and can't be routed around.

33
00:02:00.845 --> 00:02:06.557
The slide lists each source,
from OMB M-22-09 to the logging

34
00:02:06.557 --> 00:02:11.482
baseline in M-26-14 and the
long-standing DNSSEC requirement.

35
00:02:11.482 --> 00:02:14.721
One point on FedRAMP: it
certifies a cloud service,

36
00:02:14.721 --> 00:02:19.144
not an architecture, and the agency
still authorizes its own system.

37
00:02:19.144 --> 00:02:21.945
A Moderate certification
doesn't cover High,

38
00:02:21.945 --> 00:02:25.957
and on-premises appliances sit
inside the agency's own boundary.

39
00:02:25.957 --> 00:02:32.164
Every chapter follows one sequence:
inventory, coexist, migrate authority,

40
00:02:32.164 --> 00:02:38.370
federate the clouds, decommission AD
DNS and DHCP, then harden and evidence.

41
00:02:38.370 --> 00:02:43.150
Five decisions carry most of the risk,
and we'll meet each one along the way.

42
00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:48.800
Module 1 is split-brain DNS and
leaving Active Directory DNS.

43
00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:52.692
For twenty years AD was quietly
the agency's DNS service,

44
00:02:52.692 --> 00:02:57.600
and the records it kept for itself
don't follow identities to the cloud.

45
00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:00.222
So leaving it is an
inventory problem first,

46
00:03:00.222 --> 00:03:03.850
finished before the first
domain controller retires.

47
00:03:04.600 --> 00:03:07.274
Split-brain is the same
name returning different

48
00:03:07.274 --> 00:03:09.688
answers depending on
where the query starts.

49
00:03:09.688 --> 00:03:11.800
Designed split-horizon is fine.

50
00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:15.940
The accidental kind is the problem,
and with several DNS systems

51
00:03:15.940 --> 00:03:19.612
holding copies of the same
names, it's close to guaranteed.

52
00:03:19.612 --> 00:03:23.800
The classic example: an Azure storage
account with a private endpoint.

53
00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:26.943
A workload in AWS sees
the private address only

54
00:03:26.943 --> 00:03:29.925
if a forwarding rule
sends that name to Azure.

55
00:03:29.925 --> 00:03:34.802
If the rule is missing, the name still
resolves, just to the public address,

56
00:03:34.802 --> 00:03:38.298
and Microsoft attributes
roughly seventy percent

57
00:03:38.298 --> 00:03:41.712
of its private-endpoint
403 errors to exactly that.

58
00:03:41.712 --> 00:03:46.338
The same silent drift hits SaaS
sign-in paths and security block lists.

59
00:03:46.338 --> 00:03:49.238
Runbooks can't keep up,
because nothing errors.

60
00:03:49.238 --> 00:03:52.390
The fix is structural: a control plane.

61
00:03:53.140 --> 00:03:58.178
When a domain is deployed, its name
becomes a DNS zone, its domain controllers

62
00:03:58.178 --> 00:04:03.615
become the authoritative servers, and
every domain-joined machine points at them.

63
00:04:03.615 --> 00:04:07.665
Nobody chose that as a DNS
architecture, but it is one.

64
00:04:07.665 --> 00:04:12.649
Storing zones in AD brings secure dynamic
updates and multi-master operation,

65
00:04:12.649 --> 00:04:15.103
and both disappear with the directory.

66
00:04:15.103 --> 00:04:20.965
So the AD DNS estate breaks into seven
components, each needing its own decision:

67
00:04:20.965 --> 00:04:24.074
integrated zones,
file-backed zones, stubs,

68
00:04:24.074 --> 00:04:29.227
conditional forwarders, GlobalNames,
reverse zones and signed zones.

69
00:04:29.227 --> 00:04:34.007
One correction to the source: conditional
forwarders are easy to export,

70
00:04:34.007 --> 00:04:36.715
because Windows stores each one as a zone.

71
00:04:36.715 --> 00:04:38.490
The hard part isn't finding them.

72
00:04:38.490 --> 00:04:42.776
It's deciding which of the forwarders
built up over the years for partners,

73
00:04:42.776 --> 00:04:45.520
trusts and retired systems still matter.

74
00:04:46.270 --> 00:04:50.162
Domain controllers advertise
themselves through DNS.

75
00:04:50.162 --> 00:04:53.547
Netlogon registers a family
of SRV and A records,

76
00:04:53.547 --> 00:04:56.932
and clients use them to
find a domain controller.

77
00:04:56.932 --> 00:05:01.545
Those records are dynamic, changing
as controllers and sites change.

78
00:05:01.545 --> 00:05:07.155
Lose them, and the failures are broad:
domain join, sign-in and Group Policy break,

79
00:05:07.155 --> 00:05:09.744
Kerberos falls back to NTLM or fails,

80
00:05:09.744 --> 00:05:13.283
and clients end up at a
distant domain controller.

81
00:05:13.283 --> 00:05:18.856
The program builds the inventory from two
sources and resolves each critical record

82
00:05:18.856 --> 00:05:21.211
from every DNS server and every site,

83
00:05:21.211 --> 00:05:26.470
and Chapter 10 makes thirty days of clean
resolution a gate for decommissioning.

84
00:05:26.470 --> 00:05:31.826
Because these records need secure
registration from domain controllers, they

85
00:05:31.826 --> 00:05:37.020
stay on AD DNS or a DDI platform that
accepts it until the last controller goes.

86
00:05:37.020 --> 00:05:39.990
Azure Private DNS can't host them.

87
00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:45.409
Clients keep their own DNS records
current through dynamic update, and the

88
00:05:45.409 --> 00:05:50.228
secure version records an owner for each
name, so only that owner can change it.

89
00:05:50.228 --> 00:05:53.240
That only exists in AD-integrated zones.

90
00:05:53.240 --> 00:05:55.122
The DHCP server is part of this,

91
00:05:55.122 --> 00:05:59.565
and a handful of settings decide
whether the records stay trustworthy.

92
00:05:59.565 --> 00:06:05.033
For example, the DHCP server that registers
a name becomes its owner, so failover

93
00:06:05.033 --> 00:06:10.915
partners must share the same DNS credential,
ideally one dedicated standard account.

94
00:06:10.915 --> 00:06:14.815
Name protection stops one client
overwriting another's name.

95
00:06:14.815 --> 00:06:19.124
And without scavenging, DNS becomes
a map of where devices used to be;

96
00:06:19.124 --> 00:06:22.971
Microsoft documents that
mismatched scavenging and lease

97
00:06:22.971 --> 00:06:26.665
times can send one computer's
Kerberos tickets to another.

98
00:06:26.665 --> 00:06:30.030
These settings are rarely
documented outside the servers,

99
00:06:30.030 --> 00:06:32.180
so they belong in the inventory.

100
00:06:32.930 --> 00:06:36.595
Microsoft's path is a phased
move toward cloud-first,

101
00:06:36.595 --> 00:06:40.942
not an all-at-once decommission,
and AD itself isn't deprecated.

102
00:06:40.942 --> 00:06:44.192
What changes is where the
DNS records come from.

103
00:06:44.192 --> 00:06:48.405
Entra ID publishes no zones
and no locator records.

104
00:06:48.405 --> 00:06:52.379
Entra-joined devices use tokens;
hybrid-joined devices still

105
00:06:52.379 --> 00:06:56.430
look for a domain controller,
and Kerberos survives in pockets.

106
00:06:56.430 --> 00:07:00.574
Here's the failure to recognize:
resolve a name to the wrong host,

107
00:07:00.574 --> 00:07:05.755
and a valid ticket for computer A gets
presented to computer B, which rejects it.

108
00:07:05.755 --> 00:07:09.830
It looks like an identity
problem, but the cause is DNS.

109
00:07:09.830 --> 00:07:12.195
So before any domain controller goes,

110
00:07:12.195 --> 00:07:16.619
keep locator records resolvable
until every consumer is token-based,

111
00:07:16.619 --> 00:07:22.090
test and prune the forwarders, and move
each remaining zone type by its own method.

112
00:07:22.840 --> 00:07:27.877
Module 2 covers edge interception,
DNS identity and DNSSEC.

113
00:07:27.877 --> 00:07:31.136
Every policy control assumes
the query reaches it,

114
00:07:31.136 --> 00:07:35.403
so we'll look at three ordinary ways
it doesn't, none of which reports.

115
00:07:35.403 --> 00:07:38.368
Then why a perfectly valid certificate can

116
00:07:38.368 --> 00:07:41.790
still fail because of
DNS, and finally DNSSEC.

117
00:07:42.540 --> 00:07:48.128
SD-WAN edges can intercept DNS no matter
which resolver a client was given.

118
00:07:48.128 --> 00:07:52.178
In the book's Cisco example,
that's the redirect-dns action.

119
00:07:52.178 --> 00:07:54.907
The point of this slide
is that the destination

120
00:07:54.907 --> 00:07:57.566
decides whether agency
policy applies at all.

121
00:07:57.566 --> 00:08:01.048
Sent to an agency resolver,
the query gets block lists,

122
00:08:01.048 --> 00:08:06.036
validation and logging, and internet
lookups leave through Protective DNS.

123
00:08:06.036 --> 00:08:10.390
Sent to an outside resolver, it gets
none of that, and nothing alerts.

124
00:08:10.390 --> 00:08:16.452
The feature has limits too; it handles
only IPv4 over UDP, for instance.

125
00:08:16.452 --> 00:08:20.779
Two refinements: the target must
be a server the edge can address,

126
00:08:20.779 --> 00:08:22.902
not a loopback agent on a laptop.

127
00:08:22.902 --> 00:08:28.332
And even a FedRAMP-listed SASE resolver
bypasses the agency's own plane, so the

128
00:08:28.332 --> 00:08:34.290
redirect target is always the agency
resolver, with any SASE resolver behind it.

129
00:08:35.040 --> 00:08:38.267
The most common bypass
is the endpoint itself,

130
00:08:38.267 --> 00:08:42.003
sending encrypted DNS straight
to a public resolver.

131
00:08:42.003 --> 00:08:44.758
Federal policy wants encrypted DNS,

132
00:08:44.758 --> 00:08:48.891
but terminated at agency
resolvers or Protective DNS.

133
00:08:48.891 --> 00:08:51.240
Each transport gives
you a different handle.

134
00:08:51.240 --> 00:08:53.827
Plain DNS can be redirected.

135
00:08:53.827 --> 00:08:59.402
DNS over TLS or QUIC can be blocked
except to approved resolvers.

136
00:08:59.402 --> 00:09:03.474
DNS over HTTPS hides inside
ordinary web traffic,

137
00:09:03.474 --> 00:09:06.453
so an edge-only design can't govern it.

138
00:09:06.453 --> 00:09:08.277
That's why the endpoint matters.

139
00:09:08.277 --> 00:09:12.386
On Windows, add the agency's
resolvers to the known DoH list,

140
00:09:12.386 --> 00:09:16.409
and note that since June 2026
the Windows Server 2025 DNS

141
00:09:16.409 --> 00:09:21.116
Server accepts client DoH, though
its upstream stays unencrypted.

142
00:09:21.116 --> 00:09:23.546
Browsers resolve names themselves,

143
00:09:23.546 --> 00:09:28.860
so the program adopts CISA's browser
configuration as mandatory policy.

144
00:09:29.610 --> 00:09:32.298
The third bypass sits on the access layer.

145
00:09:32.298 --> 00:09:37.264
A rogue DHCP server handing out a
malicious resolver can move a whole

146
00:09:37.264 --> 00:09:41.709
subnet onto an attacker's DNS in
one step, past every block list.

147
00:09:41.709 --> 00:09:46.035
In IPv6, rogue router advertisements
do the same, and they're often

148
00:09:46.035 --> 00:09:50.360
the bigger surface, because many
networks don't govern them at all.

149
00:09:50.360 --> 00:09:55.798
Other DHCP options steer traffic too, like
the search list and the proxy setting.

150
00:09:55.798 --> 00:09:58.761
The Windows safeguard is
narrower than it looks:

151
00:09:58.761 --> 00:10:03.798
it stops a misconfigured Windows server,
not a home router plugged into a wall port.

152
00:10:03.798 --> 00:10:07.567
That takes DHCP snooping and
RA Guard on the switches,

153
00:10:07.567 --> 00:10:13.222
central option management, and IPAM so
an unexpected lease is visible at once.

154
00:10:13.222 --> 00:10:15.688
And since prevention is never complete,

155
00:10:15.688 --> 00:10:19.780
watch for hosts whose queries
never reach the governed logs.

156
00:10:20.530 --> 00:10:25.741
DNS identity, the authoritative mapping
of names to addresses and owners,

157
00:10:25.741 --> 00:10:28.218
is the first link in the trust chain.

158
00:10:28.218 --> 00:10:31.890
Certificates, policies
and tokens all name their

159
00:10:31.890 --> 00:10:35.742
subject by FQDN and trust
DNS to get the caller there.

160
00:10:35.742 --> 00:10:40.593
Moving to Entra ID tokens removes the
domain controller but adds names that must

161
00:10:40.593 --> 00:10:45.154
resolve consistently, like the right
sign-in endpoint for the cloud you're in.

162
00:10:45.154 --> 00:10:48.471
Here's the certificate
version of split-brain:

163
00:10:48.471 --> 00:10:54.143
a CA's revocation names exist only in AD
DNS, and a cloud caller can't resolve them.

164
00:10:54.143 --> 00:10:58.269
One client breaks the handshake,
another quietly skips the check,

165
00:10:58.269 --> 00:11:01.555
nothing is logged, and the
ticket goes to the PKI team.

166
00:11:01.555 --> 00:11:02.968
That's Decision 5.

167
00:11:02.968 --> 00:11:06.691
And for regional naming,
certificate wildcards are narrow,

168
00:11:06.691 --> 00:11:09.020
so use per-region names instead.

169
00:11:09.770 --> 00:11:13.582
DNSSEC lets a resolver verify
that an answer is authentic.

170
00:11:13.582 --> 00:11:15.579
It doesn't encrypt anything;

171
00:11:15.579 --> 00:11:20.182
NIST is explicit that DNSSEC and
encrypted DNS are complements.

172
00:11:20.182 --> 00:11:25.420
Federal policy has required DNSSEC
for agency domains since 2009.

173
00:11:25.420 --> 00:11:30.289
On internal zones, NIST's new guidance is
more cautious than the whitepaper was:

174
00:11:30.289 --> 00:11:35.383
signing them is likely of limited value,
and recommended against unless required.

175
00:11:35.383 --> 00:11:40.486
So the program signs external zones
always, validates on every agency resolver,

176
00:11:40.486 --> 00:11:45.358
and signs internal zones only where
the system's authorization requires it.

177
00:11:45.358 --> 00:11:46.983
The platforms aren't equal.

178
00:11:46.983 --> 00:11:51.078
Windows Server's default zone-signing
key is below NIST's floor,

179
00:11:51.078 --> 00:11:53.283
so the program uses stronger keys.

180
00:11:53.283 --> 00:11:56.836
And Azure DNS and Route 53
sign public zones only,

181
00:11:56.836 --> 00:12:01.020
so neither gives the agency
validation it controls.

182
00:12:01.770 --> 00:12:06.182
Operating DNSSEC is mostly key
management, automated and audited.

183
00:12:06.182 --> 00:12:10.431
NIST recommends a hardware security
module for the important keys,

184
00:12:10.431 --> 00:12:13.313
signatures valid for
about five to seven days,

185
00:12:13.313 --> 00:12:17.333
and it prefers ECDSA, because
smaller keys keep responses small.

186
00:12:17.333 --> 00:12:19.408
The slide has the full parameters.

187
00:12:19.408 --> 00:12:24.020
Moving a signed zone has its own
careful sequence, run in Phase 3.

188
00:12:24.020 --> 00:12:26.420
Don't start a key
rollover during the move.

189
00:12:26.420 --> 00:12:31.103
Keep the old signing until the new chain
validates from an independent checker,

190
00:12:31.103 --> 00:12:33.445
and only then stop signing at the source.

191
00:12:33.445 --> 00:12:37.708
Watch the validators, because
clients only see generic failures.

192
00:12:37.708 --> 00:12:41.698
An AD-signed internal zone
moving to Azure Private DNS,

193
00:12:41.698 --> 00:12:45.069
which can't sign, is a
documented risk decision.

194
00:12:45.069 --> 00:12:49.710
And retire old trust anchors after
the ninety-day validation period.

195
00:12:50.460 --> 00:12:55.723
Part 2 builds and runs the control plane,
starting with Module 3, the design.

196
00:12:55.723 --> 00:13:00.847
Every cloud ships its own private DNS
and its own address manager, and unless

197
00:13:00.847 --> 00:13:06.330
one plane governs both, names and addresses
drift apart one console at a time.

198
00:13:07.080 --> 00:13:12.115
Each cloud ships its own private DNS,
with its own console, API, zone model

199
00:13:12.115 --> 00:13:17.405
and failure behavior, and none was
designed to share policy with the others.

200
00:13:17.405 --> 00:13:22.980
The slide compares AWS, Azure,
Google Cloud and VMware NSX.

201
00:13:22.980 --> 00:13:25.540
Azure private endpoints
are the most common

202
00:13:25.540 --> 00:13:28.030
source of split-brain,
so they get the detail.

203
00:13:28.030 --> 00:13:30.349
The private answer only comes back from a

204
00:13:30.349 --> 00:13:32.805
resolver that can see
a linked private zone.

205
00:13:32.805 --> 00:13:34.755
Three behaviors catch teams out.

206
00:13:34.755 --> 00:13:39.530
DNS answers don't grant access;
the service's network setting does.

207
00:13:39.530 --> 00:13:44.418
Use one zone per service type everywhere,
with Microsoft's recommended names.

208
00:13:44.418 --> 00:13:49.525
And watch the NXDOMAIN trap: a partner's
storage account can lead into your empty

209
00:13:49.525 --> 00:13:54.920
zone and fail, which is fixed by enabling
fallback to internet on the zone's link.

210
00:13:55.670 --> 00:13:59.583
Two rules turn Azure private
endpoints into something governed.

211
00:13:59.583 --> 00:14:01.669
First, forward the public zone.

212
00:14:01.669 --> 00:14:06.547
Every resolver outside Azure, on-premises,
in other clouds and at branches,

213
00:14:06.547 --> 00:14:10.625
forwards each service's public
zone, not the privatelink zone,

214
00:14:10.625 --> 00:14:15.582
to the Private Resolver's inbound
endpoint or an agency resolver that does.

215
00:14:15.582 --> 00:14:18.023
Azure Government uses different names,

216
00:14:18.023 --> 00:14:20.982
so a design spanning both
clouds needs both sets.

217
00:14:20.982 --> 00:14:26.288
Second, build the resolver in the hub as
a forwarding bridge, not an authority,

218
00:14:26.288 --> 00:14:31.508
so on-premises DNS reaches it by
conditional forwarding, never delegation.

219
00:14:31.508 --> 00:14:33.896
The slide carries the sizing limits.

220
00:14:33.896 --> 00:14:38.845
The traps are worth remembering: don't
link a ruleset to the network its own rule

221
00:14:38.845 --> 00:14:40.140
points at, or it loops,

222
00:14:40.140 --> 00:14:44.160
and don't list a custom resolver
beside Azure's built-in one.

223
00:14:44.910 --> 00:14:48.147
The address side has the same
problem as the name side.

224
00:14:48.147 --> 00:14:51.688
Every cloud and NSX allocates
addresses on its own,

225
00:14:51.688 --> 00:14:57.335
so a range created in a console can overlap
on-premises space or another division,

226
00:14:57.335 --> 00:15:00.285
and nobody knows until a conflict appears.

227
00:15:00.285 --> 00:15:02.210
Integration depth varies.

228
00:15:02.210 --> 00:15:06.953
AWS can hand authority for private
address scopes to an external IPAM,

229
00:15:06.953 --> 00:15:10.510
with Infoblox as the book's
example, but not in GovCloud,

230
00:15:10.510 --> 00:15:13.198
so it doesn't help a
GovCloud estate today.

231
00:15:13.198 --> 00:15:17.535
Azure and Google Cloud can't hand
off, so you mirror and reconcile.

232
00:15:17.535 --> 00:15:23.063
The rule: reserve blocks in the enterprise
IPAM before any cloud or NSX network is

233
00:15:23.063 --> 00:15:25.226
created, integrate where you can,

234
00:15:25.226 --> 00:15:28.510
reconcile where you can't,
and monitor sync health.

235
00:15:28.510 --> 00:15:32.200
And plan IPv4 and IPv6 together.

236
00:15:32.950 --> 00:15:35.875
Chapter 08's architecture
is simple to draw.

237
00:15:35.875 --> 00:15:40.017
A branch query goes through the
SD-WAN edge to an agency resolver,

238
00:15:40.017 --> 00:15:43.690
into the policy plane, and on
to each cloud's private zones,

239
00:15:43.690 --> 00:15:47.675
while internet lookups leave
through CISA's Protective DNS.

240
00:15:47.675 --> 00:15:53.200
Five decisions carry almost all of the
operational risk, and each fails silently:

241
00:15:53.200 --> 00:15:57.900
the query still resolves, just outside
policy or to the wrong address.

242
00:15:57.900 --> 00:16:02.563
They are the redirect-dns destination,
the order of the forwarding chain,

243
00:16:02.563 --> 00:16:06.139
per-cloud conditional
forwarding, the AD DNS inventory,

244
00:16:06.139 --> 00:16:10.025
and reachability of the
certificate revocation endpoints.

245
00:16:10.025 --> 00:16:13.379
Each has a check you run
in the live configuration,

246
00:16:13.379 --> 00:16:17.387
not the design document, before
the next SD-WAN or DDI change.

247
00:16:17.387 --> 00:16:21.547
For example, resolve one name
per namespace from every origin;

248
00:16:21.547 --> 00:16:24.440
a public address means a missing rule.

249
00:16:25.190 --> 00:16:27.490
The policy plane isn't one box.

250
00:16:27.490 --> 00:16:32.582
It has three parts: an on-premises DNS
and DHCP layer that replaces AD DNS,

251
00:16:32.582 --> 00:16:36.035
a management plane holding
the system of record,

252
00:16:36.035 --> 00:16:41.127
and local resolvers that keep
answering if management is unreachable.

253
00:16:41.127 --> 00:16:44.503
It overlays the cloud services;
it doesn't replace them.

254
00:16:44.503 --> 00:16:47.517
Changes converge in
seconds, not milliseconds,

255
00:16:47.517 --> 00:16:51.691
so an emergency block has to be
verified where clients are served.

256
00:16:51.691 --> 00:16:53.523
Infoblox is the book's example,

257
00:16:53.523 --> 00:16:57.190
and its government cloud offering
is certified at Moderate.

258
00:16:57.190 --> 00:17:00.409
The architecture stays the
same in federal clouds;

259
00:17:00.409 --> 00:17:03.015
authorization and integrations change.

260
00:17:03.015 --> 00:17:07.308
A Moderate plane doesn't cover High
systems, so use a service authorized

261
00:17:07.308 --> 00:17:11.165
at the right level, or keep the
plane inside the agency's boundary.

262
00:17:11.165 --> 00:17:14.350
The slide lists the other
government-cloud gaps.

263
00:17:15.100 --> 00:17:18.187
Module 4 is the migration
and running the plane.

264
00:17:18.187 --> 00:17:22.321
Name resolution has to keep
working while its authority moves,

265
00:17:22.321 --> 00:17:25.660
so Chapter 10 makes the
migration a series of small,

266
00:17:25.660 --> 00:17:30.588
reversible cutovers, each proven from
every origin before the next starts.

267
00:17:30.588 --> 00:17:34.210
Then Chapter 11 covers
operations and evidence.

268
00:17:34.960 --> 00:17:38.514
Here's the book's sequence
applied to moving

269
00:17:38.514 --> 00:17:41.772
off AD DNS and DHCP onto one policy plane.

270
00:17:41.772 --> 00:17:45.957
Six phases: inventory,
coexistence, migrating authority,

271
00:17:45.957 --> 00:17:49.697
cloud federation,
decommissioning, and hardening.

272
00:17:49.697 --> 00:17:54.710
Each one ends with a rollback point and
a consistency test, not a calendar date.

273
00:17:54.710 --> 00:17:58.635
The playbook's durations are planning
estimates; the gates decide.

274
00:17:58.635 --> 00:18:01.610
And every move of authority
runs the same loop.

275
00:18:01.610 --> 00:18:03.572
Lower the TTL well ahead.

276
00:18:03.572 --> 00:18:06.973
Export, transform and
import with a dry run.

277
00:18:06.973 --> 00:18:09.034
Validate from every origin.

278
00:18:09.034 --> 00:18:11.548
Switch authority one mechanism at a time.

279
00:18:11.548 --> 00:18:14.436
Wait until no client
queries the old server.

280
00:18:14.436 --> 00:18:17.623
Then restore the TTL
and file the evidence.

281
00:18:17.623 --> 00:18:20.211
If a check fails, you revert the switch,

282
00:18:20.211 --> 00:18:24.000
and the old authority,
still intact, answers again.

283
00:18:24.750 --> 00:18:28.104
Phase 1 is read-only, so it
carries no rollback risk,

284
00:18:28.104 --> 00:18:31.213
but anything it misses
can't be repaired later.

285
00:18:31.213 --> 00:18:37.059
It collects every zone, record, forwarder,
DHCP scope and resolution path, including

286
00:18:37.059 --> 00:18:42.651
the health of the data, because stale
records migrate as faithfully as good ones.

287
00:18:42.651 --> 00:18:45.487
Then each component gets
a target and a method.

288
00:18:45.487 --> 00:18:47.988
Internal zones move to the new platform.

289
00:18:47.988 --> 00:18:51.751
External zones need a registrar
change, so they're high risk.

290
00:18:51.751 --> 00:18:55.537
And locator records stay where
domain controllers can register,

291
00:18:55.537 --> 00:18:58.062
because there's no
Azure-native equivalent.

292
00:18:58.062 --> 00:19:03.210
Phase 2 adds the new resolvers without
moving authority, forwarding both ways,

293
00:19:03.210 --> 00:19:07.962
with exactly one authoritative path
per namespace so queries don't loop.

294
00:19:07.962 --> 00:19:12.210
Two errors cause most failures
here, and the slide names both.

295
00:19:12.960 --> 00:19:18.038
Phase 3 is where zones, forwarders,
DHCP scopes and clients actually move,

296
00:19:18.038 --> 00:19:21.085
each move one pass
through the cutover loop.

297
00:19:21.085 --> 00:19:24.767
Records move, then you observe
for at least thirty days,

298
00:19:24.767 --> 00:19:28.285
with the old copy left in
place as the rollback point.

299
00:19:28.285 --> 00:19:32.847
Three patterns cover almost every
zone, and each has its own rollback.

300
00:19:32.847 --> 00:19:36.902
Check the record types that behave
differently on the new platform;

301
00:19:36.902 --> 00:19:41.029
SRV records are the most consequential
and the most underestimated,

302
00:19:41.029 --> 00:19:43.635
because nothing moves
them when AD retires.

303
00:19:43.635 --> 00:19:49.310
DHCP moves scope by scope, with its
options, and failover stays on throughout.

304
00:19:49.310 --> 00:19:51.560
Clients move last, in waves.

305
00:19:51.560 --> 00:19:55.922
One trap to plan for: removing a
network link from an Azure private zone

306
00:19:55.922 --> 00:20:01.160
deletes its auto-registered records, and
Microsoft says they can't be restored.

307
00:20:01.910 --> 00:20:06.711
Phase 4 brings each cloud under the
plane, and its real contribution is the

308
00:20:06.711 --> 00:20:11.748
validation matrix: every class of name
resolves correctly from every origin.

309
00:20:11.748 --> 00:20:15.183
A private endpoint name
returning a public address from

310
00:20:15.183 --> 00:20:18.984
anywhere is a failed test, even
if the application seems to work.

311
00:20:18.984 --> 00:20:23.221
Phase 5 is the one step whose
mistakes can't be cheaply undone,

312
00:20:23.221 --> 00:20:25.085
so it's gated, not scheduled.

313
00:20:25.085 --> 00:20:30.361
All five gates must pass, including locator
records resolving at one hundred percent

314
00:20:30.361 --> 00:20:32.144
for thirty consecutive days,

315
00:20:32.144 --> 00:20:35.860
and ninety days of parallel
running with no critical alerts.

316
00:20:35.860 --> 00:20:39.689
Then decommission in order,
archiving the final exports

317
00:20:39.689 --> 00:20:43.110
before anything is removed,
with a seven-day burn-in.

318
00:20:43.110 --> 00:20:49.150
Losing locator records early causes
forest-wide sign-in and domain-join failures.

319
00:20:49.900 --> 00:20:53.518
A single policy plane only works
if divisions can still run their

320
00:20:53.518 --> 00:20:57.338
networks through it, because a division
that can't will work around it.

321
00:20:57.338 --> 00:21:00.670
So each division gets its
own scope, with manager,

322
00:21:00.670 --> 00:21:04.587
operator and auditor roles,
and no visibility outside it.

323
00:21:04.587 --> 00:21:08.421
Whatever stays on Windows or
Azure needs the same discipline,

324
00:21:08.421 --> 00:21:10.601
including locks on critical zones.

325
00:21:10.601 --> 00:21:14.881
Every object should sit behind
one documented API, so automation,

326
00:21:14.881 --> 00:21:18.925
ticketing and pipelines share
the same roles and audit trail.

327
00:21:18.925 --> 00:21:20.075
Then telemetry.

328
00:21:20.075 --> 00:21:25.858
M-26-14 doesn't name DNS or DHCP, but
its baseline needs network address

329
00:21:25.858 --> 00:21:31.438
information, searchable for six
months and retrievable for a year.

330
00:21:31.438 --> 00:21:36.150
The book's reading is that query logs
and lease history supply exactly that.

331
00:21:36.150 --> 00:21:38.580
Last, how to keep it all honest.

332
00:21:39.330 --> 00:21:43.042
Last, keeping it honest during
and after the hybrid period.

333
00:21:43.042 --> 00:21:47.068
Chapter 11's troubleshooting
reference covers the common failures.

334
00:21:47.068 --> 00:21:51.904
For example, a private endpoint returning
its public address usually means

335
00:21:51.904 --> 00:21:56.204
an unlinked zone, a bad forwarder
target or a timeout that's too short.

336
00:21:56.204 --> 00:22:01.493
Then watch continuously: resolve the
locator records every fifteen minutes from

337
00:22:01.493 --> 00:22:04.292
every site, detect
drift between platforms,

338
00:22:04.292 --> 00:22:07.480
and commit each report so
the history is evidence.

339
00:22:07.480 --> 00:22:11.830
The evidence checklist opens with the
five decisions, each checked live.

340
00:22:11.830 --> 00:22:17.145
So, before the next SD-WAN or DDI change:
audit every branch's redirect target,

341
00:22:17.145 --> 00:22:22.545
verify the chain order, finish the AD
DNS inventory before touching a domain

342
00:22:22.545 --> 00:22:25.974
controller, confirm the
sovereign-cloud limits,

343
00:22:25.974 --> 00:22:28.717
and take open questions to the vendors.

344
00:22:28.717 --> 00:22:29.540
Thank you.
